              "text": "The iconic scene where Del Boy falls through the bar occurs in the episode titled **\"Yuppy Love\"**. \n\nHere are the key details and trivia about this legendary moment, widely regarded as one of the funniest scenes in British television history:\n\n### **Episode Details**\n* **Episode Title:** *Yuppy Love*\n* **Season & Episode:** Series 6, Episode 1\n* **Original Air Date:** January 8, 1989\n* **The Plot:** Inspired by the movie *Wall Street*, Del Boy (David Jason) decides to adopt an upwardly-mobile \"yuppie\" lifestyle, complete with a red braces look, a Filofax, a mobile phone, and a smart coat\u2014though he still drives his trusty yellow three-wheeler.\n\n---\n\n### **The Scene**\nDel Boy and Trigger (Roger Lloyd-Pack) ditch their usual pub, *The Nag's Head*, to hang out at a trendy, upscale wine bar to try and impress women. \n\nSpotting two attractive women across the room, Del leans in to Trigger and says his legendary line: **\"Play it nice and cool, son, nice and cool...\"**\n\nAs Del attempts to casually lean back against the bar to strike a suave pose, he fails to notice that the bartender has just lifted up the bar hatch to walk through. Del falls completely flat on his back through the open hatch, disappearing instantly from view.\n\nThe comedy is perfected by **Trigger's reaction**. Completely oblivious to the fall, Trigger continues looking around the room, utterly baffled as to how Del vanished into thin air in a split second. \n\nDel slowly climbs back up, tries to salvage his dignity, and tells a clueless Trigger, **\"Drink up, Trig, we're leaving.\"** As they walk out, Del blames Trigger for the embarrassment, muttering, *\"You're cramping my style.\"*\n\n---\n\n### **Behind-the-Scenes & Fun Facts**\n\n* **Inspired by Real Life:** Writer John Sullivan got the idea for the scene when he was in a pub and watched a man nearly fall through an open bar hatch in the exact same way. \n* **Added at the Last Minute:** The scene was not part of the original script structure. Because *Yuppy Love* was the first episode to transition from a 30-minute format to a 50-minute format, the episode was initially running about five minutes short, and the bar fall was written in to pad out the time.\n* **Trigger Wasn\u2019t Originally in the Scene:** According to rehearsal scripts that surfaced years later, Trigger was not originally written to be in that specific scene. Actor Roger Lloyd-Pack reportedly showed up on set that day, and Sullivan decided to write him in. Trigger's delayed, confused reaction ultimately became one of the funniest elements of the entire sequence.\n* **Perfect Comic Timing:** David Jason spent a lot of time rehearsing the fall with a stunt coordinator to ensure he fell perfectly horizontally without looking down or anticipating the drop, making the sudden disappearance look entirely natural and hilariously abrupt. \n* **Filming Location:** The scene was filmed at an underground venue called **The Old Granary** on Queen Charlotte Street in Bristol, which was dressed up to look like a trendy London wine bar.",
